Z||000024||Voytek||12-18-2001||05:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B>Ray, sure the shoe would be on the other foot if it was a woman walking in on her husband, boy friend.<P>Swingers have basically come to a mutal consensus within their relationships and have gotten over their respective issues of jealousy.<P>Hardcore swingers, and to which some have had their marriages last 40+ years after shakier times in a monoagamouse relationship before turning to this have learned that just because they are married, their spouses are not their property.  Basically, its their body...not yours...not mine.<P>Marriage.  Yes individuals exchange vows.  Based on a long standing principle set fourth over many years of how we are "supposed" to act within these predefined, socially arranged, relationships. Governing rules.  However, are we meant to be monogamous?  I don't think so.  From the male standpoint, I'm with the belief, that we "will" seek out partners to pass our lineage on down the stream.  Women seek to find a mate that has strong attributes so her young will be strong.<P>Okay, my wife and I have been married only three years now.  Do I intend on cheating on her?  No...even though some of my posts may suggest that.  Its all in fun, but I am a little more liberal and open then my wife as she is very conservative and old fashioned in her beliefs.  Sometimes, I tease her that she is a Prude on certain issues where she is "OH NO!" and I'm like "AWSOME!".  <P>Even though I grew up under a governing principle of sexuality that was enforced by my parents, if they ever even talked about sex, as well as our church which preached that if you crossed pinkys while holding a song book while in church with a girl, you were having sex.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  Yes it was a Catholic Church.<P>To each his own.  What ever flips their boat.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The shoe fits both ways, J.  And it's not about jealousy, either.  I think you're missing the whole point of marriage.<P>It's also not about property.  Sure, thinking that your spouse is your property is definitely not right, however, have you ever heard the words 'being faithful'?<P>Yes, once you're married you <B>are</B> meant to be monogamous.  ESPECIALLY once you're married.  I won't go into pre-marital sex - that may have been discussed in the Harry Potter thread (it's so long that I don't even remember).<P>Is your wife reading this?  You say you won't cheat on her.  Reading your comments, I find that hard to believe.  I mean, what's stopping you?  Once you're tired of her in a few years, your beliefs may surface and off you'll go.  I mean, it's YOUR body, right?<P>You make the words 'old-fashioned' and 'conservative' sound quite negative.  There is nothing wrong with maintaining the sanctity of marriage - if not for one's self, than for one's kids.  And if no kids are involved, than for your spouse.<P>I've been married for 3 years.  I would never marry any woman with your belief system.  Life has enough hassles as it is - finding one's wife with someone else is the last thing anyone needs.||||reg||1
